# spawn — the effect port `:exec`-family bindings ride, declared once and
# reused across every domain's hexagon (mirrors execution.port / payment.port,
# the gem's own bundled ports — this one lives in the consuming corpus
# because `spawned_by` is a hecks_playground_conception vocabulary word, not a
# hecksagain-upstream one yet).
#
# Signal is :effect (async, matches charged_by's shape) — the domain composes
# the call (`Aggregate.spawned_by("AdapterName", on: "TriggeringEvent") do
# success "..." ; failure "..." end`) and the runtime's record_effect_outbound
# (hecks-hecksagain, runtime/dispatcher.rb) durably hands it to the
# OutboundEvent outbox — ONE Record per subscribing adapter, delivered
# out-of-process by bin/adapter-host, never the core itself.
#
# UNCONDITIONAL, unlike charged_by : the :exec family this port replaces
# never branched (exec.hecksagon's own vision : "routes the result into
# :result_into as a follow-on dispatch", one target, no success/failure
# distinction). A spawned_by bind is written with success == failure ==
# the SAME command (typically Cascade.RecordResult) to express that —
# OutboundEvent already carries both fields, so no new IR shape was needed.
#
# i780 (2026-08-17) — the design proposal this implements. See there for
# the full :exec -> spawned_by re-expression rationale.
Hecks.port "spawn" do
  verb   "spawned_by"
  signal :effect
end
